{"name":"Bear's grease hair pomade","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117563-1.png/960px-A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117563-1.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117563-1.png","caption":"A treatise on bear's grease Fleuron T117563-1","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Bear's grease is rendered bear fat used as a scented pomade for hair. In Europe it was sold as a remedy for baldness and a styling dressing; Native American groups used it to make hair shine.","The appearance payoff was glossy, styled hair, and claimed regrowth. Historical claims were not tested in modern trials."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["The fat was washed, melted and strained, then scented; later commercial pots were sold in chemist shops. Users applied it to hair daily."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No modern safety data. Historic products were often adulterated with pork fat or lard."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117563-1.png/960px-A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117563-1.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117563-1.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"A treatise on bear's grease Fleuron T117563-1","license":"Public domain","creator":"Ross, Alexander, perfumer","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117563-1.png","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/A_treatise_on_bear%27s_grease_Fleuron_T117